<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n37509" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">praedĭātus</span></b> , a,
		um, adj.
		id.. <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<i>Possessing landed properly</i> (post-class.): 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">bene praediatus,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">App. Flor. p. 349, 40</span></i>
			.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense2"><b>B.</b> In
			gen., <i>wealthy</i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">in omnibus praediatus,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Mart. Cap. 1, § 46</span></i>
			.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			<i>Who pledges his lands to the State</i>: praediatus
				<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ὑπὲρ φόρου δήμου
			ἐνδεδεμένος</span>, Gloss. Philox.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
    <div class="alpheios-lex-alph-source">From <u>
            <u>A Latin Dictionary</u>
        </u> (Charlton T. Lewis, Charles Short, Oxford, Clarendon Press, 1879) </div>
